cartridge "MYCROFT MEWS/JOHN R. PRAGER/1985"

' MYSTERY AT MYCROFT MEWS - a whodunit for 1 to 6 detectives
' A ColecoVision game based on "Mystery at Mycroft Mews" by John R.
' Prager, Ahoy! October 1985 (Commodore 64, BASIC).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' listing, orig/mycroft.bas), the game is rebuilt for the ColecoVision: a
' drawn lane of sixteen shops at night, a detective who walks from door to
' door, a gliding hand in the menus, sound effects and an original tune.
'
' The game: someone in the Mews has been murdered. Each turn a detective
' walks to one of the sixteen buildings (the map keeps their places
' secret until visited), sees who and what is there, and does one thing:
' search, interrogate a suspect (three questions), take, drop or hide an
' item, read the coroner's report (morgue), examine records (law office,
' clinic, bank), read the victim's papers, fingerprint an item or accuse
' (police station). To win, accuse at the police station with the right
' victim, murderer, weapon and motive; a wrong accusation puts the
' detective out of the game. Suspects wander between turns.
'
' From the original: the twelve suspects, their hobbies, the twelve
' weapons (four ways to kill), the ledger / will / diary and the three
' motives (blackmail, inheritance, madness), the master key, magnifying
' lens and police badge at the police station; the victim's building
' locked except to the key holder; weapons hidden at their owner's place
' (the murder weapon never at the murderer's), the papers hidden at the
' victim's; searching finds each hidden item 6 times in 10 (always with
' the lens); one item carried; suspects answer truthfully 3 times in 4,
' refuse 1 time in 5 unless you hold the badge, and the murderer always
' denies owning the weapon or that it was the weapon; every answer table
' of lines 2200-4090 with the listing's odds; alibis (the murderer's is
' never a confirmable one), bank levels, will and clinic files (lines
' 230-290); suspects move at random after each round, the owner stays at
' home while a detective is in his building; a false arrest eliminates;
' the solution at the end.
'
' Liberties:
' - Hand-the-controller flow instead of secret codes. The C64 asked each
'   player for a two-key secret code on the shared screen so nobody could
'   take another's turn; on one controller that protects nothing. Before
'   each turn (with two or more detectives) a blank screen asks to pass
'   the controller to DETECTIVE #n and press the button when ready, and
'   at the end of a turn the screen is cleared before the next hand-off,
'   so no one's findings stay on screen.
' - Solo play: one detective plays alone (the listing allowed 1 player);
'   the game counts the rounds and gives a rating when the case is solved.
' - The map labels a building with a short name once any detective has
'   entered it (the C64 showed nothing: players had to remember the
'   shuffled places), and names it on the bottom line under the walker.
' - The walker hops from door to door (one building per stick tap)
'   instead of drifting in quarter steps.
' - Fixed listing bugs: "<suspect> OWNS" always named the elephant gun
'   when truthful (A=-1*YN): here the truthful answer names a weapon the
'   suspect owns (or the new car if none). "I THINK THAT PLAYER #X HAS"
'   picked X from 1-57 when lying: here from the players in the game.
' - Text re-wrapped for 28 columns; the C64's jingles replaced by own
'   sounds; menus driven by the stick, YES / NO as a menu.
'
' Controls: either controller. Map: the stick walks to the next building
' (left, right, up, down), a side button enters. Menus: up/down and a
' button. Interrogation: the last line switches between asking about
' suspects and about items.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects
' after; the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always
' given. Own xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes.

